2026-11-19 03:43:40 - Agent_20261119033811 - INFO - [+] Agent signaled completion: Reviewed all 5 results of the py/jinja2-loader-paths query (CWE-022) in jinja2 3.1.2. All 5 were submitted as FALSE POSITIVES, because the path-traversal hypothesis does not hold for this codebase revision:

1) src/jinja2/loaders.py:195 (FileSystemLoader.get_source) and src/jinja2/loaders.py:337 (PackageLoader.get_source) — both pass the untrusted template name through split_template_path(), which is the dedicated traversal guard. It splits on "/" and raises TemplateNotFound if any segment contains os.path.sep, os.path.altsep, or equals os.path.pardir (".."), and normalizes away "." and empty segments. Confirmed by the library's own tests (tests/test_loader.py:114-117: `pytest.raises(TemplateNotFound, split_template_path, "../foo")`). Absolute paths are also neutralized: "/etc/passwd" yields ["etc","passwd"] — a relative subpath, not an absolute one — and PackageLoader additionally applies os.path.normpath after posixpath.join. get_source() calls split_template_path() BEFORE any join/open, so no traversal segment reaches the sink.

2) src/jinja2/loaders.py:346 (open in PackageLoader) — sink is reached only with the path built from the sanitized pieces plus the trusted, developer-supplied package template_root.

3) src/jinja2/bccache.py:268 (FileSystemBytecodeCache.load_bytecode -> open) — the filename derives from self.pattern % bucket.key, where bucket.key is a SHA-1 hexdigest from get_cache_key() (bccache.py:150-159), not attacker text. The template name is only hashed, so no traversal characters survive. The directory comes from the developer-supplied config, and _get_default_cache_dir() performs owner/permission checks (_unsafe_dir()) on the created dir.

4) src/jinja2/utils.py:162 (open_if_exists) — a generic utility whose filename argument is a caller-supplied path; within this group its only relevant caller is FileSystemLoader.get_source, which already received a sanitized posixpath.join of the trusted searchpath and the split_template_path pieces. It is also gated by os.path.isfile(), so no traversal primitive is introduced here.

No new independent vulnerabilities were identified during the review. Note that split_template_path's guarantee is on-segment based; on POSIX the check is sound, and cross-platform concerns (e.g. Windows drive/UNC) are explicitly handled by the intentional use of posixpath.join plus normpath in PackageLoader, as documented in the code comments. Application-level risks remain if a developer builds a custom BaseLoader without calling split_template_path, but that is a usage pattern outside this library's code and not a defect in these locations.
